  "notes": "London's quietest, most analog modern entry, produced by a single multi-instrumentalist. The band is the catalogue spine the entry folds out — 55–125 \"weighted very slow\", which is why the mode sits at 84 despite the ceiling — and the keys are the named B♭ minor, D♭ major, F♯ minor and F major. The drums are the entry's sentence: \"a played kit recorded dry, close and quiet — kick on 1 and 3, snare soft on 2&4 with a short decay, hats barely present, tambourine or shaker carrying the subdivision, and frequent full drop-outs. The feel is unquantised and slightly loose.\" So `quantizeStrength` drops to 0.5 with a real snare displacement, the hat fill density is 0.08, the velocity tiers are soft, and the tambourine-and-shaker layer is four to seven hits a bar — busier than the kit it accompanies. Harmony is \"hymn-and-70s-soul: 4-to-8-bar cycles with real cadences, maj7/m9 colours, gospel passing chords, and modulation into the final section — written on piano\", so no families are authored (the entry gives no numerals), the extension spread is seventh-led, `susOrDimProb` carries the passing chords and the harmonic rhythm keeps moving. Melody is \"soft mid-alto at conversational volume, very low note-density, long phrases with almost no melisma, and lyrics delivered as direct address; the choir stack is the dynamic climax rather than a belt\" — hence density 2–4, a legato long arc, and an octave-stacked counter for the choir. Bass is \"round and melodic, moving in 8ths with passing tones; occasional upright\". ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number and all melody figures; the research sources the producer relationship, instrumentation and tempo spine but not these values.",
